/*
 * COMBO_AK_CONSISTENCY_HACK
 * =========================
 *
 * What it is: a 36-key combo (ALL_KEYS) on l_alpha_aks (layers 0-24), binding &none,
 * timeout hold_key_event_delay (11 ms). Nobody presses 36 keys in 11 ms, so it never fires.
 * Needs CONFIG_ZMK_COMBO_MAX_KEYS_PER_COMBO=36 (keyboards/chocofi/corne.conf).
 *
 * What it really does: every key on layers 0-24 now has a combo candidate, so every press
 * is captured and held by the combo engine. A key is held by its LONGEST live combo:
 * 11 ms on the 8 hack-only keys (X, C, F, RT0, the 4 thumbs), 16 ms on most, 18 ms on A E I H.
 * A key-up, or a press that shares none of its combos, releases it earlier.
 *
 * Why it matters for AKs: &ak types its letter at delivery D and turns the AK layer on at
 * D+10 (ak_tap_time, one queue tick). A follower delivered before D+10 lands on the base layer.
 *
 * Rolls, with the hack (t = trigger press, gap = follower press - t):
 * - gap <= 9 ms: the follower shares the hack with the trigger, so both stay held and leave
 *   together at the trigger's 11 ms mark. The trigger's letter is stamped "now"; the follower
 *   keeps its old timestamp, so ZMK's quick-tap check (require-prior-idle-ms = -1) waves it
 *   straight to the keymap, on BASE. M->T at 5 ms types "mt", C->P at 6 ms types "cp".
 * - gap >= 11 ms (pairs sharing no real combo): the trigger is already out, or the follower's
 *   press flushes it; the follower then waits out its own hold (>= 11 ms from its own press),
 *   so it arrives after the AK layer is on. Adapts up to the window edge (~105-115 ms).
 * - gap = exactly 10 ms: predicted stall on hardware (follower waits for the next key event,
 *   then usually adapts late). Not measured; native tests cannot show it.
 *
 * The trade, over the 54 compiled AK pairs (23 AK layers); board bands, predicted from native tests:
 * - Rescues 11 pairs whose follower has no combo of its own (X, C, F, R thumb), for 11-25 ms gaps.
 *   Without the hack those pairs miss up to 25 ms.
 * - Costs 34 ordinary pairs every roll <= 9 ms. Without the hack they never miss (below the edge):
 *   C->P at 6 ms adapts to "tp".
 * - 6 pairs that share a real combo with their trigger (L->G, H->E, M<->W, D<->V) keep the same
 *   outcome with or without it: fragile up to 14-16 ms (M<->W / D<->V: only timing moves, below).
 * - A real 2-key combo typed under 11 ms, when it is the only real combo left, fires at its
 *   16 ms deadline instead of on completion.
 *
 * Upstream ZMK v0.3+ silently drops combos with >= 20 keys, so there this behaves like "hack off".
 *
 * The invariant that matters:
 *   ak_tap_time (10) < max(hold_key_event_delay, follower's combo timeout)
 * The margin at the start of the band is max(11, follower's combo) - 10: 1 ms on hack-only
 * followers, 6 ms on 16 ms followers.
 *
 * Full explanation: Design/ak-combo-hack.md. Evidence (source reading + 181 native tests):
 * Design/Timing/ (README.md, FACTS.md, MODEL.md, PAIRS.md).
 *
*/ 
#define COMBO_AK_CONSISTENCY_HACK \
  COMBO_LAY_BASE(l_alpha_aks, ak_consistency_hack, &none, ALL_KEYS, hold_key_event_delay)


COMBO_AK_CONSISTENCY_HACK